<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palmyrene Empire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palmyrene Empire was a breakaway state centred on the Syrian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, which controlled much of the Roman East in about 270–273 CE under Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>. It arose during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crisis-of-the-third-century">Crisis of the Third Century</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> fragmented into three competing polities. Emperor Aurelian reconquered it in 272–273 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palmyrene-empire.jpg" alt="Palmyrene Empire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ennomus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire_of_Palmyra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the murder of the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Alexander Severus</a> in 235, generals fought over the empire while the frontiers were raided by Carpians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Alemanni and attacked by the Sassanids. Shapur I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-edessa">Battle of Edessa</a> in 260 and captured the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>. The Palmyrene leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a>, nominally loyal to Valerian's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a>, raised an army of Palmyrenes and Syrian peasants and beat Shapur near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. In 261 he defeated the usurpers Quietus and Macrianus. He received the title Governor of the East and styled himself King of Kings.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> and his son Hairan were assassinated in 267, by a cousin according to Joannes Zonaras and the Historia Augusta, which names him Maeonius. His ten-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> inherited the throne, with his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> as regent. She took the real power, kept her husband's titles, secured the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and pacified the Tanukhid tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the spring of 270 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> set out with her generals Septimius Zabbai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabdas">Septimius Zabdas</a>. Zabdas sacked Bosra and secured Roman Arabia. In October 270 a Palmyrene army of 70,000 invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and declared Zenobia queen of Egypt. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenagino-probus">Tenagino Probus</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> but was besieged at the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and killed himself after capture. In 271 Palmyrene forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a> in Galatia, the empire's furthest extent, although an attempt on Chalcedon failed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> at first kept up a show of subordination, issuing coins in the name of the emperor Aurelian with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> as king, but toward the end of 271 Vaballathus and his mother took the title of Augustus. In 272 Aurelian marched through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, defeated Zenobia at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-immae">Battle of Immae</a> and again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>. Zenobia left to seek help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> but was caught near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and the city capitulated. Most high-ranking officials were executed, and the fates of Zenobia and Vaballathus are uncertain. In 273 the Palmyrenes rebelled under Septimius Apsaios and proclaimed a relative of Zenobia, Antiochus, as Augustus. Aurelian spared Antiochus but razed the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Historians disagree about the revolt's motive. Andreas Alföldi saw native ethnic opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Irfan Shahîd a pan-Arab movement, and Mark Whittow a reaction to Rome's inability to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. Warwick Ball argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> were contenders for the Roman throne, and Fergus Millar holds that the evidence is not yet enough to decide. Modern Syrian nationalists have treated the empire as a Syrian state that sought to free the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> from Roman rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palmyrene Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…weakness of an imperial system without a reliable succession mechanism: in those five decades some two dozen recognised emperors and many more usurpers claimed the purple, and for a time the empire fragmented into three competing polities, the Gallic Empire, the <b>Palmyrene Empire</b> and the central Roman state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After fighting with the Roman prefect Tenagino Probus, the Palmyrenes gained control of Alexandria and the province, making it part of the <b>Palmyrene Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-egypt">Palmyrene Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
